WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.220
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.310
So we continue our discussion
now of electron and proton

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:14.210
scattering.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.450
And we dive deep into
the structure using

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:19.100
deep inelastic scattering.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.110
Inelastic here means
that we are destroying

00:00:21.110 --> 00:00:23.840
the structure of the proton
in the scattering process.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.900
But we have a way to look
at the remnant of the proton

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.540
and also of the
scattered electron,

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:32.210
and then compare our
theoretic expectation

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:35.930
for the cross-sections with
the finding in experiments.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.440
Let me just talk about
this in more general terms.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.500
The energy of the probing
electron or the photon

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.990
in the scattering
process allows us

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:48.710
to look at the proton
with varying resolution.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.810
So at very low
energies, we basically

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:52.280
see a point-like particle.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.960
And then the scattering
process looks

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.180
very much like the scattering
of an electron with a muon.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:59.460
If we increase the
energy of the electron,

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.390
we can see that there's
an extended charge

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.560
distribution in the proton.

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:07.580
Further increase allows
us to resolve the fact

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:11.330
that the proton is made
out of three quarks.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.220
And if you increase
the energy further,

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.400
we see a lot of new
particles appearing, quarks

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:19.940
and antiquarks and
gluons, which make up

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:21.710
the structure of the proton.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.430
The picture here is--

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:29.450
I like this very much, I drew
this myself some years ago--

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:33.800
is what I would like
to have you remember.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.930
So in deep inelastic
scattering experiments,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:39.830
we basically use the
photon scattered--

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:42.350
radiated off the
electron as a magnifying

00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.070
glass for the proton.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.910
So we can look into the
structure of the proton here,

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:50.030
and we see the distribution
of the charged particles

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:50.930
in the proton--

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:52.890
only the electrically-charged
particles.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:55.340
We don't have
scattering between--

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.130
direct scattering between
photons and gluons.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:59.660
So in measurements,
what we can do

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:02.600
is we can test
scattered electron,

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:05.608
and we can look for the
remnant of the proton

00:02:05.608 --> 00:02:08.150
in our measurement, and then we
do differential cross-section

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.759
measurements, compare
with our theory,

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:15.450
and can infer information about
the structure of the proton.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.350
To do this, we have special
kinematic variables which

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:20.910
turn out to be very useful.

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.590
The most important
one is probably

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:26.130
this x year, which is called
the Bjorken scaling x or Bjorken

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.280
x, which you can think about--

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.330
so this is q squared,
the momentum transfer

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:34.380
of the photon, p is the
momentum of the proton,

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:38.580
q is the momentum transfer,
this q here, factor of 2.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.340
And what this is basically
the fraction of the momentum

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.230
carried by the parton here
in the scattering process.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.880
There's a few other
useful variables,

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.900
but I don't want to go into
any of the details yet.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.570
So there's a number of
very important scattering

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.080
experiments.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.180
The first one I mentioned
before is SLAC-MIT experiment,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.060
which led to the discovery
that the proton is made out

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.630
of quarks, and the Nobel
Prize in Physics 1990

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.430
to Jerry Friedman, Henry
Kendall, and Taylor.

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:13.410
And what they did
is they basically

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:19.020
had a beam at SLAC of
electrons of 5 to 20 GeV.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.700
And they scattered this beam
off of hydrogen target protons.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.250
And they used this
spectrometer here

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:28.320
in order to then make a
differential measurement

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.290
of the scattered electrons.

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.580
So that's very cool.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.530
Even higher energies
were available

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:38.630
at HERA, the
electron-positron collider,

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:40.230
where the energies
of the electrons

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:46.650
were in the order of 30 GeV
of the protons up to 830 GeV.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:48.900
And so what we find then
in those collisions,

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:52.450
the differential cross-section
measurements, is shown here.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.820
And it's very-- not
an easy plot to read.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.940
So you see our
structure functions

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.190
here in the logarithmic plot.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.430
Remember, this is the
a log 10 plot here.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.240
And you see here q squared,
the momentum transfer

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:08.150
of the photon, so the energy
used in the scattering process.

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.750
When we try to
read this here, we

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:13.290
can look at a fixed q
squared, for example.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:20.680
And at a fixed q squared,
you see that if you probe--

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:26.880
if you are testing for a
fixed fraction of the partons

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.660
taking away partons, a
fraction of the parton's

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.110
momentum of the proton, you see
that the lower the fractions,

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.560
the more particles you see.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:42.360
So at a fixed energy, you
see many, many more particles

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.760
the lower you go
in the fraction.

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:47.040
So there seem to be like
an increase of particles

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.350
the lower the fraction is.

00:04:49.350 --> 00:04:55.830
If you then check for a fixed
fraction, let's say 0.4--

00:04:55.830 --> 00:05:00.360
it means that the parton carries
40% of the proton's energy--

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.870
you see that it's almost flat
as a function of q squared.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.200
So it seems like there's 40%--

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.903
the number of particles you
see at 40% of momentum fraction

00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:12.070
is constant, this q squared.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:16.620
However, if you look at smaller
energies-- sorry, smaller

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:20.670
momentum fractions, you
see that higher energies

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:25.080
seem to show even more particles
at this momentum fraction.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.680
And the ways to understand
this is two diagrams.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.280
So the first one
is this one here,

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:34.620
where you see a quark
radiating a gluon.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.040
And so what you see is
that the deeper you look,

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.830
you are able to then
resolve this part here.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:49.670
And so you see more
quarks and gluons

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:51.890
which carry even smaller
momentum fractions

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.840
than the initial quark here.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.570
You also see diagrams
like this, which

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:59.810
is called gluon splitting,
as a gluon splits

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:01.640
into a quark/antiquark here.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.480
And you start resolving those.

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.620
And those also carry
lower momentum.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.750
The evolution of our--

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.660
of this parton distribution
function, or the structure

00:06:11.660 --> 00:06:14.180
functions, can be
calculated and described

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:18.620
in the so-called
DGLAP equations.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.670
And all you do here is
calculate the contributions

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:24.772
from the so-called quark
and gluon splitting.

00:06:24.772 --> 00:06:26.480
So you calculate the
splitting functions,

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:31.660
these higher-order corrections
to a very simple quark model.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.645
And you find that you can
actually very nicely describe

00:06:34.645 --> 00:06:35.950
those curves here.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:37.835
So you see this yellowing--

00:06:37.835 --> 00:06:40.090
yellow here is
the QCD fit, which

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:44.620
basically uses those
splitting functions as input.

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:45.550
All right.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.820
So we learned quite a bit
about this proton already.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.000
If we want to now
calculate a cross-section

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:55.210
of a proton scattering
with a proton,

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.720
we are actually interested
in the energy distributions

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.320
or the momentum distributions
of the partons in the protons.

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.390
And so we'll come back
to how we use this later.

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.960
But I want to introduce parton
distribution functions which

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:09.363
do exactly that.

00:07:09.363 --> 00:07:10.780
They're defined
as the probability

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.010
to find a parton in
the proton that carries

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:16.780
energy between x and x plus dx.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:21.190
You can write them using the
structure functions before.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:24.680
But they literally
describe this probability.

00:07:24.680 --> 00:07:26.770
And so what you find
inside the protons are now

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.280
the valence quark, the down
quark and the up quarks,

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.830
c quarks and
antiquarks, and gluons.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:36.130
So you want to describe
those momentum distributions

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:39.480
or energy distributions
of those particles.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.430
There's a number of sum rules.

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:44.820
If you integrate momentum
fractions from 0 to 1,

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:48.510
1 being the momentum of the
proton, if you integrate them

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:50.580
all together, you
have to find 1,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:54.600
because that is the momentum
of the proton you start with.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:57.510
If you integrate the down
quarks and the up quarks,

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:00.630
you find 1 or 2,
meaning that those

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:03.360
are the number of valence
quarks we have available.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.370
If you integrate
the distributions

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:09.960
of strange and antistrange
and charm and anticharm,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:12.840
you get 0, because
there needs to be

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.200
the same number of
strange and antistrange

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.990
and charm and antistrange.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.760
And because of
energy conservation,

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:24.970
the sum needs to-- this sum,
those sums need to be 0.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.220
And then we can look at those.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:30.300
So what's shown here is x
times the Parton Distribution

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:33.270
Functions, the PDFs,
as a function of f.

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:37.230
And what you see here
for our valence quarks,

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:40.080
you see a distribution which
is kind of what you expect--

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.210
it almost peaks at 0.3, a
surge, and has the distributions

00:08:45.210 --> 00:08:47.010
because there's
kinematics involved,

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:49.950
and also interactions--

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.100
because of the interactions
with the gluons.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:57.600
And then you see the c
quarks and antiquarks.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.780
And you see them increasing
in numbers quite significantly

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:04.080
here as you go to small fraction
of the momentum carried.

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.761
It's exactly what we just
discussed in the previous plot

00:09:06.761 --> 00:09:09.530
already.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.320
An interesting way to look at
this very same distribution

00:09:12.320 --> 00:09:17.690
function is if you plot them
proportional to the momentum

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:19.980
fractions, or the area
proportional to the momentum

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:20.480
fractions.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:24.260
And what you see there is
that a very significant part

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:28.150
of the momentum of the proton
is carried by the gluon.

00:09:28.150 --> 00:09:31.610
So you see here again, our
valence quarks, our c quarks,

00:09:31.610 --> 00:09:34.070
and the gluons itself.

00:09:34.070 --> 00:09:37.250
So that's all I wanted to say
on the structure of the proton.

00:09:37.250 --> 00:09:39.900
We'll later in a lecture see
how we can use those PDFs,

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:42.170
those Parton Distribution
Functions, in order

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:46.370
to calculate a cross-section
in proton scattering.

